[Music]  the cuddly morris and the powerful great  protector are just two of the incredible  creatures who play a big role in marvel  studios shanxi and the legend of the ten  rings to learn more about how they were  brought to life i'm speaking with vfx  supervisor christopher townsend hey  chris hey how you doing  this film brings so many new creatures  into the marvel cinematic universe how  did you work with the visual development  team to envision these characters and  then actually bring them to life with  vfx  so working with the vis-dev department  at marvel they would start creating some  of the characters the creatures the  looks the environments of sort of  general guidelines of this is where we  want you to go  we take that initial artwork and then  start developing it into a grounded  realistic looking creature  that's quite a different challenge and  that's i think where visual effects  comes in which is so exciting  so then how do you take a fantastical  creature like morris or the great  protector and make them feel as real as  the other characters on screen i think  it's very easy to design something as a  cgi character that is otherworldly but  it's very difficult to relate to it and  to therefore believe it so you always  have to try and find things that you can  grab hold of as an audience because you  never want the audience to sort of step  outside of the film you always want them  to be sort of fully engaged  well i know that morris stole my heart  how did you work with the team to create  his overall look and characterization  morris was a very interesting character  to work with his sort of  characterization is that he is a  faceless six-legged winged furry  creature so yeah trying to find a way  for that character to emote and look  cute and friendly without a face has  been incredibly challenging we obviously  looked at different animals  out there in the real world is he like a  bear the way he walks or is he like a  dog on tiptoes is he like a horse when  he trots or you know what does a  skeleton look like and so there were an  awful lot of animation tests that we did  early on to try and figure out the  mechanics of how he moved the other  thing you have to really consider is how  the feathers work and the wings and so  we're looking at ostriches and other  strange looking creatures trying to  really figure out not only the  ergonomics and the mechanics but the  physiology and the aesthetic to try and  make it feel as real as possible  so when morris is in a scene with  another character what are the actors  using as reference so during filming  obviously one of the key things is  trying to explain to the actors exactly  what it is they would be looking at the  way it was manifested on set was a big  green cushion that the actors had to  lift up and hold and stroke and prod we  had morris on a stick we were able to  sort of puppeteer him across the floor  to act as an eye line  so once you get that footage from set  how do you go about actually bringing  morris to life the first thing is  tracking it in relation to the camera  and in relation to the world and in  relation to the actor you can then start  replacing the cushion with a cg  character that you've created the big  challenge then is the interaction and  obviously with morris particularly he's  got fur and he's got feathers and so we  would have our actors be touching the  green form and then we would replace  that green form with our furry character  and then it's a matter of lighting it  and rendering it so that it looks  exactly like the lighting on the actors  and the set pieces around them and it's  a very very careful process of  compositing that character into the  scene correctly within the set and under  the fingers of all that kind of stuff  and it takes many many weeks months to  do an individual shot to get it to look  as real as it does in the film  [Music]  so the other creature we gotta talk  about is the great protector when you  saw the great protector in the script  what went through your mind  i thought to myself i can't believe i'm  gonna be making a movie about a dragon  that's so cool this was an interesting  character in many ways because it wasn't  the normal traditional western dragon it  doesn't have wings it doesn't breathe  fire you'll notice in the film that  whenever you see this particular  character she is always moving she's  always slithering and sliding along  through the air we looked at eels and  water snakes of how they traveled  through water and used a lot of that as  reference for our movement so once you  understand the movement what kind of  prep goes into a scene where you know  the actors are going to be interacting  with this creature one of the great  things about  this particular dragon is we have  characters actually riding it because of  that we had to build a what we would  call a buck  and we went to our visual effects  company who was working on the dragon we  then asked them to create a simplified  version of just the head portion  particularly in a small portion of the  body that people were able to hold onto  and sit on and sort of interact with so  putting this whole piece onto a gimbal  so that it rotates and spins and moves  and tilts and everything and then we  photograph our actors sitting on this  thing with wind and sometimes rain to  imply that they were going through water  and then we added big camera moves  trying to emulate the sense that they  were flying it's very very challenging  to try and make that stuff look real but  i think you know it's it's incredibly  fun challenge  so once you have that footage what kind  of processes does it need to go through  with vfx the challenge particularly once  we'd actually got this photography was  then putting that into our world of  tallow which is where the dragon exists  in this sort of mythical place we also  have to figure out where the camera is  in relation to the body of the dragon  and then tracking the dragon so the  dragon now has a cg dragon and then on  top of the real world camera that we  have we then add additional camera  movements and we then start building our  cg world around that um and start  putting in all other action beats  now looking back at this incredible film  and all of the characters you got to  help create what was your favorite part  of working on this project i've been  very fortunate in my career working with  marvel to do several origins films and  they're always really fun there's an  overall aesthetic to the film that leans  into some aspects of mcu sort of history  but also the great thing about an origin  story is you get to develop your own  look and i'm looking forward to seeing  what an audience thinks of the film  thank you so much chris and of course  everyone go see marvel studios shangqi  and the legend of the 10 rings in  theaters now  [Music]  you

